>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked November 2026"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Whey Protein Concentrate</Fragment>
      Concentrate is the cheaper, slightly less filtered form of whey. Here is what WPC80 actually contains, who should skip it, how to tell when an "isolate" tub is mostly concentrate, and six picks priced per 25 g of protein.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#what-is-wpc80">What WPC80 Is</a>
      <a href="#concentrate-vs-isolate">Concentrate vs Isolate</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#spot-the-blend">Spotting Blends</a>
      <a href="#lactose">Lactose Tolerance</a>
      <a href="#muscle">Muscle and Recovery</a>
      <a href="#unflavored">Using Unflavored WPC</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Purity</a>
      <a href="#skip-concentrate">When to Skip It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best whey protein concentrate for most people is NOW Sports Whey Protein Concentrate if you want the lowest cost per gram with a third-party sport certification, or Naked Whey if you want a one-ingredient, grass-fed tub. Concentrate, usually sold as WPC80, is whey that has been filtered less than isolate: it keeps a few more grams of fat and lactose per 100 g, and in exchange it is usually cheaper to make. For anyone who digests dairy without trouble, that trade is worth taking. This guide explains what is in the scoop, where the savings are real and where they are not, and how to read a label that says "isolate" on the front and "concentrate" first in the ingredient list. We built it from label panels, retailer prices checked in early November 2026 and published research. We did not run taste or mixing tests ourselves.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-is-wpc80" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What WPC80 Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey starts as the watery liquid left over when milk is turned into cheese. That liquid is mostly water, with a small amount of protein, lactose, minerals and fat. To make a powder, processors run it through membrane filters that hold back the larger protein molecules and let water, lactose and minerals pass through, then spray-dry what is left. Stop the filtering early and you get whey protein concentrate. Keep going, usually with a second microfiltration or ion-exchange step, and you get whey protein isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            "Concentrate" covers a wide range. The US Dairy Export Council describes whey protein concentrates running from roughly 34 percent protein up to the high 80s on a dry basis. The number after the letters tells you which grade you are buying: WPC34 is a food ingredient used in baking and processed foods, while WPC80 is about 80 percent protein and is the grade almost every sports-nutrition tub uses. When a brand says "whey protein concentrate" without a number, it is very likely WPC80, and the label math in the next section lets you confirm it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other 20 percent or so of a WPC80 powder is what separates it from isolate. Typical supplier spec sheets put WPC80 at around 4 to 8 percent fat, 3 to 8 percent lactose, a few percent minerals and a few percent moisture. Isolate is held to at least 90 percent protein, with fat and lactose usually under 1 percent each. In a 30 g scoop, that difference works out to perhaps 1 to 2 extra grams of fat and 1 to 2 extra grams of lactose for concentrate. That is the whole story for most people: a little more milk, a little less filtration.
          </p>

          <p>
            Concentrate also keeps more of whey's minor components, such as lactoferrin, immunoglobulins and milk phospholipids, because less of the original whey is stripped out. Brands sometimes market this as a health advantage. The amounts are small, the research on them comes mostly from isolated, purified fractions at much higher doses, and nobody has shown that the leftovers in a scoop of WPC80 do anything measurable for an ordinary healthy adult. Treat it as a neutral detail, not a reason to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            What concentrate is not is a lower-quality protein. The amino acid profile of WPC80 and isolate is close to identical per gram of protein, both are complete proteins, and both are digested quickly. The difference is how much non-protein material rides along with each gram, which matters for lactose sensitivity and very tight macro budgets and hardly at all for muscle building. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> comparison covers the processing in more depth if you want it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concentrate-vs-isolate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Concentrate vs Isolate: Lactose, Fat and Protein per Scoop
          </h2>

          <p>
            The cleanest way to compare the two forms is to put unflavored versions side by side, because flavor systems, cocoa and sweeteners muddy the macros. The table below uses label panels for single-ingredient unflavored powders, taken at the time of writing; values vary by lot and by flavor, and brands round to the nearest gram or half-gram.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product (unflavored)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fat</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Carbs</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein share of scoop</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Whey (WPC)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">83%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Nutricost Whey Concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">33 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">76%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NOW Sports Whey Concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">33 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">73%</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NOW Sports Whey Isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">28 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">86%</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two things stand out. First, the gap is small. Per serving, the concentrates carry about 2 g of fat and 1 to 3 g of carbohydrate, most of which is lactose, against 0 and 1 g for NOW's isolate. That is about 10 to 20 extra calories a scoop. If you drink one shake a day, it adds up to roughly the calories in a slice of cheese over a week, which is not the reason anyone's diet works or fails.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, the protein share of the scoop is the tell. A single-ingredient isolate should land in the mid-80s or higher once you divide label protein by scoop weight, because the label reports protein as eaten rather than on a dry basis and a little moisture always remains. A single-ingredient concentrate lands in the 70s or low 80s. You can use this ratio on any unflavored product to check what is actually in the tub, regardless of what the front says.
          </p>

          <p>
            For flavored products the ratio drops for both forms, because cocoa, flavorings, gums and sweeteners take up weight. A chocolate isolate might show 75 to 80 percent, and a chocolate concentrate blend 65 to 70 percent. The useful comparison then is between flavored products, or between a brand's flavored and unflavored versions of the same base.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where isolate genuinely earns its premium is for people who react to lactose, people tracking fat to the gram on a contest diet, and anyone mixing protein into clear, juice-like drinks, which need isolate or hydrolysate to stay transparent. For everyone else the scoop of concentrate does the same job for less money, which is why it remains the base of most of the best-selling powders on our <a href="/best-whey-protein-powder/">best whey protein powder</a> list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
